It is easier to calculate the probability that we get at most 5 heads. We can just use Binomial distribution with number of trials N=10 and the probability of getting H in a single trial p=0.5.
p_k = {N \choose k} p^k (1-p)^{N-k}
The probability that we get exactly 0.... 5 heads is
\begin{equation*}
\sum_{k=0}^{5}{10 \choose k} 0.5^k(1-0.5)^{10-k} = 0.376953 \, \, .
\end{equation*}
Since we want the opposite of this, we subtract from 1
\begin{equation*}
p_1 = 1-p_{\leq 5} = 1-0.376953 = 0.623047 \, \, .
\end{equation*}
So we get \, 62.30% \, probability of getting at least 6 of 10 heads.
